<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.15.1: Solving Proportions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00322</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.15.1: Solving Proportions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1e778a35-31b3-4074-8367-e04b3518f235</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minute)</title>
<para>The purpose of this warm up is to help students recall what a proportion is and how to solve a proportion. Elicit student strategies, such as scaling up/down numerators and denominators, and if students don’t bring up cross-multiplication on their own, make sure to cover that strategy.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Remind students that they may have learned more than one way to solve these equations, and it is okay to use a variety of strategies.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Solve each of the following proportions: </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \( \frac{x}{56} = \frac{7}{8} \) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=49\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \( \frac{56}{72} = \frac{y}{9} \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=7\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \( \frac{98}{154} = \frac{-7}{p} \) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(p= -11\)</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<list>
  <item>Show different work examples. Who used cross-multiplication? Who used their multiplication facts to find the missing value?</item>
  <item>Can you think of situations in real life where you use proportions?</item>
</list>
</section>
</content>
